>  Specifically, monster
>difficulty does not increase as fast.  5 or 10 or even 20 extra hp
>means nothing when a mumak has your cornered.

Mumaks are a bob and weave monster. They are slow! You simply run away from
them.

Sometimes it's hard to get out of the combat wombat groove. You figure you
simply whack on everything that comes by and it's all good. To be successful
in nethack you have to be well equipped, and very very smart. mumaks for
example cannot hurt you at all if you are not next to them. So never get
next to them.

>One of the most critical components of survival does not increase with
>XP, and that is AC.

Agreed. Securing AC is always my first short term goal in the game.

>  You need to make sure that your equipment keeps up with your XP.

Again agreed. Negative AC reduces the average damage you take per hit, with
the to hit at its absolute minimum. So getting and staying at negative AC is
critical.

>  HP can go away fast if your weapons or AC are insufficient.

That's how I died in my last three deep games. Nurses and succubi are so
dangerous because they take away your AC. I have to work at triple checking
all equipment after interacting with them. This helps keep from being
pretified by a chickatrice because you didn't put your gloves back on for
example. DOH!

>  For a new player, I'd recommend keeping your pet with
>you and keep your XL at 3 as far down as Sokoban and even into
>Minetown.

I agree on the pet. I'd even add make sure that you take the time to level
the pet up to large status. And it's easier to do if you keep your XL low.

As for artifically depressing your own XL, there's considerable room to debate.
Gaining XLs just isn't HP. It's skill slots, it's improved spellcasting. It's
better to hit. There's more power points available. You lose a lot by not 
levelling up.

I say level up carefully. For example I'd be leary of levelling up to killer
bee level if I didn't have poison resistance. 

>  I believe that the ability to level up in the early game is
>too easy for one's own good and an early hazard.

It can be if you don't understand how the game balances it out. Personally
I do the minetown protection run precisely because I force control of that
balance by doing the run. I improve my base AC, which increases my
survivability in mid game. I'm forced to level up the pet, and encouraged to
keep my own XL low for the duration of the run. Typically at the end of the
run I have a buff pet, decent, flexible AC which I can improve by taking on
more weight or keep barely negative and lightweight if I so choose. I'm much
better prepared to level up after the run than if I levelled up before the run.

But then understanding game balance is important too. Often when I run a monk
they start off with a 1st level spell and 4 power points, which isn't enough
to cast the spell. So during the run I'll actually level up to XL2 to make sure
I can cast a useful spell during the run.

>  I find that a good
>way of keeping my character above the monster curve is by using my
>pets to kill the guards in Minetown and doing the protection run at XP
>= 1,2, or 3.

Agreed. Of course you have to watch the watch captain. That dude(tte) is
bad news for pets.

>Basically, you'll find your own balance.  People ascend with many
>different stles.  My advice is that if you over and over again
>encounter monsters that are too much to handle, then you're probably
>leveling up too fast.

Agreed. Also realize that too much to handle may mean that you don't have the
right equipment or resistance at the time to handle that monster.

>  Soldier ants and centaurs with wands of speed
>monster are good tests; if they're too difficult then slow down.

They are good tests. You should be unburdened, have negative AC, and poison
resistance at a minimum to tackle soldier ants and killer bees.

Rothes are a good test too. They come in packs, are fast, and do decent
damage. If you (or you can your pet) can't dispatch them easily, then you're
either levelling up too fast or are not well equipped enough for them.

>However, once you're at Level 10 or 12 or so, you shouldn't need to
>worry about it.

Given that you are properly equipped.

>  I learned how to survive soldier ants after being
>able to do a pacifist protection run; but I learned how to ascend
>after I learned to live without pets.

Pets become optional to me after the run. Generally I'll drop them if I don't
have a good pet management tool (leash or ideally a magic whistle). If they
are domesticated my pet management strategy is to abandon them until I need
them (like needing to clean out a deep dungeon shop) then retaming.

